  "Question": "If \\(10^{\\log_{10}9}=8x+5,\\) then \\(x\\) equals:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac12\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac58\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac98\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{2\\log_{10}3-5}{8}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "By definition, \\(\\log_{10}9\\) is the exponent to which \\(10\\) must be raised to obtain \\(9\\). The logarithm is defined because its argument is positive and its base is positive and different from \\(1\\). Therefore\n\\[\n10^{\\log_{10}9}=9.\n\\]\nThe given equation becomes \\(8x+5=9\\). Subtracting \\(5\\) and dividing by \\(8\\) gives\n\\[\nx=\\frac{9-5}{8}=\\frac12.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\frac12\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
